WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.680
On July 10th, 2014, police officers in Coffee County Georgia entered a private yard

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.200
where six children were playing.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.780
The officers, who were in pursuit of a criminal suspect, ordered the kids to get facedown on the ground.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:18.200
When the family dog came out into the yard one of the officers fired at the dog twice but missed.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.400
One of those bullets struck a ten-year-old in the back of his knee.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.600
When the child's mother attempted to sue the officer for damages the case was dismissed

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.640
due to qualified immunity, a legal doctrine that effectively shields police from most civilian lawsuits.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.020
Following the police killing of George Floyd and growing calls for police accountability,

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.600
this once obscure doctrine has been thrust into the national spotlight.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.600
Qualified immunity. Qualified immunity. Qualified immunity.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.920
More people are starting to look around and take notice at what's gone wrong with policing today,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.960
and are asking gee, is it the fact that the police officers are officially above the law?

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.520
Maybe that's part of the problem.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.400
William Baude is a professor at the University of Chicago,

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.500
and the author of a widely cited paper arguing that qualified immunity should be overturned.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.480
Qualified immunity is a judge-made doctrine that says that when you sue a government official

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.780
for violating the Constitution you can't get anything.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.600
You can't get any recovery or any damages, even if they did something unconstitutional.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.880
Unless they also did something that was clearly unconstitutional or if the court has put this as

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:25.360
you can only recover damages against an officer who was plainly incompetent or who knowingly violated the law.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.560
Incompetence or that an officer acted illegally on purpose is difficult to prove in court,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.880
and there were many examples of individuals clearly harmed by police error but unable to collect damages

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:42.400
because specific issues of a case hadn't yet been clearly established as unconstitutional in a previous case.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.980
One case is thrown out because of the difference between unleashing a police dog

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:51.940
to bite a motionless suspect in a bushy ravine and unleashing a police dog to bite a compliant suspect

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.720
in a canal in the woods.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.300
If you or I or any normal person is accused of violating the law and we say,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:02.880
oh I didn't realize it was illegal, or, I knew this was a gray area but I thought I could kind of skate by.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.240
That's not an excuse. There's an old adage ignorance of the law is no excuse.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.580
And the one group of people for who it is an excuse are the government officials who enforce the law,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.640
who you'd think should be held more accountable, who if anything, are actually trained in the law

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.820
and should know what they aren't supposed to do.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:23.660
This sweeping protection was never voted on or signed into law but was instead created over decades

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:28.900
of judicial actions to give government officials room to make judgment calls without fear of lawsuits.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:35.200
In 1871 Congress passes the Civil Rights statute that we still have today the Ku Klux Klan Act.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.660
That statute doesn't say anything about immunity it says that when a government official, a state official

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.600
violates their constitutional rights you can sue them for damages.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:47.420
A hundred years later in 1967 Chief
Justice Earl Warren decides the statute

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:51.400
doesn't quite mean what it says and that
there was a kind of unwritten defense

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.800
that sort of preempts the statute and
provides these kinds of defenses.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.880
In 1982 in a case called Harlow versus
Fitzgerald they announced the modern

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.840
qualified immunity test which says you
can only sue a police officer if they

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.860
violate clearly established law.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:06.360
Then in the forty years since then there's been a drip of maybe 30 or 40 cases where the court makes the test

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:15.660
a little bit harder every time and makes it really hard to find  anything to be really established law.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.880
So because this is a judge-made doctrine the court can fix that anytime they can they could step in and say,

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.880
oh actually we you know made a mistake and the doctrine shouldn't be here,

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:32.760
or we've gone a little bit too far and we need to make more clear that isn't supposed to be impossible to recover.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.200
But Congress can also fix it so because it's just an interpretation of a statute that Congress wrote.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.440
Congress also has the

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:45.840
power to step in at any time and try to
either change the doctrine or repeal it

